m68k: a library function that returns and takes a 64-bit integer cannot be called

arch/m68k-all/include/gencall.c generates aros/m68k/libcall.h. Its QUAD
argument macros place the 64-bit argument in a register pair and then call
__AROS_LC<n>##t, where t is the declared return type. It emits the aliases for
a LONG return (__AROS_LC2LONG and up) and for a double return, and none for a
QUAD return. A function that both returns a 64-bit integer and takes one
therefore pastes __AROS_LC4QUAD, which nothing defines; the preprocessor leaves
the call as an identifier and the compiler reports

  error: expected expression before 'QUAD'

at the first argument, which is the return type rather than the real subject.
No module in the tree returns a 64-bit integer by value, so nothing exercises
it today.

A 64-bit integer comes back in D0/D1. The ordinary call macro already describes
that: it casts to a function pointer with the declared return type and lets the
compiler place the result, exactly as it does for LONG. The aliases are the
same four lines the LONG return has.

--- a/arch/m68k-all/include/gencall.c
+++ b/arch/m68k-all/include/gencall.c
@@ -514,6 +514,15 @@
 "#define __AROS_LC3LONG   __AROS_LC3\n"
 "#define __AROS_LC4LONG   __AROS_LC4\n"
 "#define __AROS_LC5LONG   __AROS_LC5\n"
+/* A 64-bit integer comes back in D0/D1, which the ordinary call macro already
+   describes: it casts to a function pointer returning the declared type and
+   lets the compiler place the result. Without these aliases the QUAD argument
+   macros paste __AROS_LC<n>QUAD, which nothing defines, and a library function
+   that both returns and takes a 64-bit integer cannot be called at all. */
+"#define __AROS_LC2QUAD   __AROS_LC2\n"
+"#define __AROS_LC3QUAD   __AROS_LC3\n"
+"#define __AROS_LC4QUAD   __AROS_LC4\n"
+"#define __AROS_LC5QUAD   __AROS_LC5\n"
 "\n"
 "#   define AROS_LDDOUBLE1(t,n,q1,bt,bn,o,s) \\\n"
 "        __AROS_LD_PREFIX t AROS_SLIB_ENTRY(n,s,o) ( \\\n"
